<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rochester, Kent</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Durobrivae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rochester is a town in Kent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where the Roman road of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/watling-street">Watling Street</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-medway">River Medway</a>. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> settlement and then the Roman town of Durobrivae, it became in 604 the seat of England's second-oldest bishopric. Its Norman castle keep and cathedral survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rochester-kent.jpg" alt="Rochester, Kent" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rochester Cathedral west facade with Norman towers viewed from Boley Hill and Epaul ClemRutter; derivative work by SilkTork · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2006SweepsCath1crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Rochester is a port town at the lowest bridging point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-medway">River Medway</a>, in the unitary authority of Medway in Kent, about 50 km east-southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. It forms a conurbation with Chatham, Rainham, Strood and Gillingham. The Romano-British name Durobrivae is commonly explained as 'stronghold by the bridge' or as a Latinisation of a British word meaning 'swiftstream'. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, writing around 730, calls it Hrofes cæster, and from this came the later forms Hrofescester, Rovescester and Rochester. Its street plan was fixed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> by the Medway, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/watling-street">Watling Street</a>, Rochester Priory and Rochester Castle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Remains from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> have been found near Rochester, which was one of the two administrative centres of the Cantiaci tribe in the Celtic period. During the Roman conquest a decisive battle was fought on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-medway">Medway</a> somewhere near it, and the first bridge was built early in the Roman period. In the later Roman period the settlement was walled in stone. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelberht-of-kent">Æthelberht of Kent</a> established a legal system that survives in the 12th-century Textus Roffensis, and in 604 the bishopric and Rochester Cathedral were founded.</p><p>The present cathedral was built between 1080 and 1130, with further work in 1227 and 1343. Rochester Castle, built by Bishop Gundulf to guard the river crossing, has one of the best-preserved keeps in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In the First Barons' War baronial forces took it from Archbishop Stephen Langton and held it against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a>, who besieged it in 1215. In 1667 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleet under de Ruijter broke through the chain at Upnor and sailed to Rochester Bridge, where it captured and burned part of the English fleet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Rochester was a city from 1211 until 1998, when it lost that status as its local government district was abolished. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> dockyard at Chatham closed in 1984 and caused a surge in local unemployment. Rochester has long been tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-dickens">Charles Dickens</a>, who owned nearby Gads Hill Place and based many of his novels on the area. The King's School, which grew out of the cathedral's foundation of 604, is recognised as the second-oldest continuously running school in the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rochester, Kent” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
